  "Question": "A rectangle with a diagonal of length \\(x\\) is twice as long as it is wide. What is the area of the rectangle?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}x^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}x^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}x^2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Let the width be \\(w>0\\), so the length is \\(2w\\). The diagonal and two adjacent sides form a right triangle because a rectangle has right angles. By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nx^2=w^2+(2w)^2=5w^2.\n\\]\nThe area involves \\(w^2\\), so we can use \\(w^2=x^2/5\\) directly without finding \\(w\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area}=w(2w)=2w^2=\\frac{2}{5}x^2.\n\\]\nThus the area is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{5}x^2}\\), choice B.",
